# FFXIV Guild Branding — Character Selection & Creative Process

## 1. Project Context

At the beginning of this project, I had only been playing **Final Fantasy XIV for approximately two weeks**.

I had never played a Final Fantasy game before, and I was still unfamiliar with most of the game's lore, characters, community culture and visual history. My knowledge of the characters was therefore initially based primarily on:

* Visual design
* In-game appearances
* Boss encounters
* Character silhouettes
* Color palettes
* Personal visual interpretation
* Early impressions from the community

This was an important condition of the project.

I was not approaching FFXIV as someone who already knew its entire universe. I was approaching it as a **designer discovering a new visual world**.

At the same time, I wanted to create a portfolio project that demonstrated much more than illustration.

The objective was to create a complete creative project involving:

* Research
* Creative exploration
* Concept development
* Art direction
* Visual identity
* Logo design
* Typography
* Color development
* Asset development
* Web design
* Final implementation

Rather than creating an unrelated fictional brand simply for the sake of the portfolio, I decided to use something I was genuinely enjoying at the time: **Final Fantasy XIV**.

The project therefore became an opportunity to combine:

> **Something I was actively interested in + a complete design and creative-development process.**

The intention was not to reproduce an existing FFXIV character as a finished illustration.

The intention was to investigate whether an existing character could serve as the foundation for an **original guild brand**, and to document the creative decisions required to transform that character into a complete visual identity.

---

# 2. Initial Direction — Calcabrina

The first major candidate was **Calcabrina**.

Calcabrina was selected because the character immediately presented an unusual visual contradiction:

> **Cute + disturbing**

The character combines:

* Doll-like forms
* Childlike visual characteristics
* Multiple eyes
* Unsettling expressions
* Horror elements
* Magical effects
* The relationship between Calca and Brina
* The fusion represented by Calcabrina

This appeared to provide strong material for a creative project.

I did not select Calcabrina without research.

The character was researched through:

* In-game appearance
* Boss encounter
* Dungeon context
* Lore
* Visual references
* Existing representations
* Color
* Character design
* Associated elements

I also began testing possible concepts and directions.

One of the strongest conceptual ideas developed from this research was the idea of three different characters emerging from a shared origin:

* **The Empty**
* **The Panic**
* **The Dissociative**

The concept explored innocent beings exposed to something horrifying and reacting to it in different ways.

The eyes also became an important visual element because of Calcabrina's terrifying glance attack.

At this stage, the project already contained genuine research and creative experimentation.

The problem was not a lack of research.

The problem was **what I chose to evaluate**.

---

# 3. What Went Wrong With Calcabrina

Looking back at the first stage, the selection process was incomplete.

I evaluated Calcabrina primarily through questions such as:

* Is the character visually interesting?
* Does it have a strong concept?
* Can I create something original from it?
* Does the character have interesting lore?
* Can I develop an interesting illustration?
* Does the contrast between cute and horror create creative possibilities?

The answer to most of these questions was yes.

However, I had not yet developed the broader criteria that later became central to the project.

I did not sufficiently ask:

> **Can this character support a complete guild brand?**

More specifically:

* Can it work as a mascot?
* Can it support a logo?
* Can its visual language extend beyond the character?
* Can it generate a coherent color system?
* Can it support typography?
* Can it create a recognizable website?
* Can it appeal to the intended audience?
* Does it provide enough room for adaptation?
* Does it demonstrate the kind of creative and branding skills I want to show in my portfolio?
* Will the identity still work when the character itself is removed?

These questions became apparent **only when I started putting the concept onto paper and attempting to develop it into an actual brand**.

This was the first major lesson of the project:

> **A character can be an excellent creative subject without being an excellent foundation for a brand.**

Calcabrina was interesting.

Calcabrina had creative potential.

Calcabrina had a strong concept.

But the project eventually revealed that I had selected the character before fully defining what I actually needed from the character.

The mistake was therefore not:

> "I chose Calcabrina without researching."

It was:

> **"I researched and tested Calcabrina, but my evaluation criteria were still incomplete, so I selected it based on the wrong set of priorities."**

This became the reason for restarting the selection process rather than forcing the original concept to work.

---

# 4. The First Major Methodological Change

The Calcabrina experience changed the project.

Instead of beginning with:

> **Which character has the most interesting concept?**

the new question became:

> **Which character can support the entire project I am trying to build?**

This introduced several new evaluation dimensions:

* Audience compatibility
* Mascot potential
* Recognition
* Silhouette
* Color identity
* Adaptability
* Branding potential
* Conceptual flexibility
* Ability to generate visual assets
* Ability to support web design
* Difficulty of reinterpretation
* Portfolio value
* Potential to demonstrate creative direction

The selection process was consequently restarted with a much broader candidate pool.

---

# 5. Candidate Exploration

The new selection phase was deliberately broad.

Candidates were considered from different visual and functional categories rather than being judged as one homogeneous group.

## Cute / Mascot Candidates

* Loporrit
* Moogle
* Tonberry
* Namazu
* Great Serpent
* Spriggan
* Mandragora
* Fat Cat
* Chocobo
* Cactuar

## Boss / Powerful Character Candidates

* Shiva
* Titania
* Susano

## Main-Character / Humanoid Candidates

* Y'shtola
* Meteion
* G'raha Tia

The purpose of these categories was not to determine winners automatically.

They were used to compare **different types of branding opportunity**.

---

# 6. Target Audience

The audience was gradually defined through the selection process.

The main target became approximately:

> **FFXIV players around their 20s, broadly covering men and women, with FFXIV as one of their main games.**

The project was not intended to specifically target:

* Very young players
* Older demographics as the primary audience
* RP-focused communities
* Sexual/RP-oriented communities
* A narrowly defined identity-based niche

This did not mean that members from those groups could not belong to the guild.

The intention was instead to create a **broad FFXIV-centered identity** capable of attracting players who enjoy the game itself.

The guild could therefore accommodate players interested in:

* MSQ
* Lore
* Characters
* Dungeons
* Trials
* Raids
* Glamour
* Housing
* Social activity
* General FFXIV gameplay

The brand should not communicate exclusively:

> "Cute characters."

nor:

> "Hardcore gameplay."

It needed enough identity to appeal to players who enjoy FFXIV as a complete game.

---

# 7. First Major Audience Insight — Avoiding Unwanted Niches

During the audience analysis, another consideration emerged.

Some guilds deliberately organize themselves around specific communities or identities and make that identity a visible part of their branding.

That was not the intended direction for this project.

The guild could naturally include LGBTQ+ players, people with different backgrounds and different interests, but the brand itself would not be built around an identity label.

Likewise, the guild was not intended to become primarily:

* An RP guild
* An ERP/RP-oriented community
* A sexual-content community
* A community centered around personal labels

The design focus remained:

> **The game, the community and the guild identity itself.**

This helped reinforce the need for a broad visual concept rather than a niche identity.

---

# 8. Cute Candidate Elimination

The cute category was gradually narrowed through visual and branding analysis.

## Loporrit

Loporrit became one of the strongest cute candidates.

Strengths:

* Very cute
* Distinct silhouette
* Good proportions
* Strong mascot potential
* White, blue and beige palette
* Clothing provides additional complexity
* Strong adaptability

However, Loporrit already has an extremely established cute identity.

The character's existing visual language makes it easy to create a cute guild brand, but this also reduces the amount of transformation required.

For the portfolio objective, this became a disadvantage.

The project needed to demonstrate **creative adaptation**, not simply produce another cute Loporrit identity.

Loporrit therefore remained valid but lost importance.

---

## Moogle

Moogle was considered visually recognizable and strongly associated with Final Fantasy.

Strengths:

* Extremely recognizable
* Strong mascot identity
* Cute
* Simple
* Immediately understandable

Weaknesses:

* Very established visual identity
* Limited room for reinterpretation
* Strong association with the traditional Moogle image
* Less opportunity to demonstrate a sophisticated visual transformation

---

## Tonberry

Tonberry was considered more unique than some other cute candidates.

Strengths:

* Distinctive silhouette
* Knife
* Recognizable personality
* Cute/strange contrast
* Strong Final Fantasy association

However:

* Brown and green dominate the palette
* It does not immediately read as "more impressive" than other candidates
* It is not necessarily the strongest cute character
* It does not have the strongest story association
* Its palette is less attractive for the intended identity
* Its overall branding potential appeared weaker than the remaining finalists

Tonberry was considered good, but not strong enough to survive the later comparisons.

---

## Namazu

Namazu was removed primarily because its visual personality did not create the desired reaction.

The character's humor and appearance did not match the desired emotional direction.

This was treated as a legitimate design-selection criterion.

The objective was not to determine whether Namazu was objectively good or bad.

The objective was to find a character that could be developed convincingly into the desired brand.

---

## Great Serpent

The Great Serpent was visually distinctive, but its teeth and serpent-like appearance created an unwanted visual reaction.

This made it less suitable as a mascot for the intended project.

Again, the issue was not objective character quality.

It was the emotional response produced by the visual language.

---

## Spriggan

Spriggan presented a similar problem.

The character was distinctive, but its furry texture and facial details created a visual response closer to discomfort than cuteness.

This made it less suitable for the intended mascot role.

---

## Mandragora

Mandragora remained reasonably interesting, but did not demonstrate enough strength compared with the stronger candidates.

It was visually usable but did not create a sufficiently strong reason to continue.

---

## Fat Cat

Fat Cat was rejected because it felt too generic when viewed outside FFXIV context.

Without knowing the game, the character could easily be interpreted simply as:

> **A generic fat cat from an anime or game.**

This weakened its ability to create a distinctive FFXIV-centered brand.

---

## Chocobo

Chocobo has enormous Final Fantasy recognition.

However, its strong association with mounts and the broader Final Fantasy mascot role made it less suitable for the intended guild identity.

It was recognizable, but recognition alone was not enough.

---

## Cactuar

Cactuar was considered too simple and too strongly mascot-oriented for the desired direction.

Its simplicity limited the number of design systems that could be developed from it.

---

# 9. Result of the Cute Category

The conclusion was not:

> Cute characters are bad.

The conclusion was:

> **Cute characters could make good guild mascots, but the strongest candidates provided less opportunity to demonstrate the level of transformation and conceptual development desired from the portfolio project.**

This caused the selection to move increasingly toward characters that could provide both:

> **recognition + creative challenge.**

---

# 10. Boss Candidates

The boss category became increasingly important because it offered the combination of:

* Strong visual design
* Music
* Memorable encounters
* Story significance
* Powerful visual identity
* Potential for an imposing guild brand

The key candidates became:

* Titania
* Shiva
* Susano

---

# 11. Titania

Titania initially appeared particularly promising.

Her design provides:

* Fairy imagery
* Strong color
* Magical atmosphere
* Femininity
* Fantasy
* Powerful boss encounter

The battle itself proved visually compelling when investigated.

Titania therefore had genuine potential.

However, she overlapped strongly with the cute/fairy territory.

This made her less differentiated once Shiva entered the competition.

Titania remained a valid candidate, but Shiva provided a broader and more distinctive branding direction.

---

# 12. Shiva

Shiva became a particularly strong candidate after the later raid appearance was encountered.

The visual reaction was immediate, but the project deliberately avoided selecting her purely from personal preference.

Her appearance was analyzed through a purely visual and technical lens.

The first ten-word interpretation was:

> **Nobility, power, refined beauty, delicacy, solitude, distance, reign, ice, aristocracy, sovereign authority.**

The important distinction was that the "power" did not necessarily mean combat power.

It suggested:

> **A monarch who possesses concentrated authority and protects her kingdom at all costs.**

The visual interpretation also suggested a distant divine figure:

> She recognizes her followers, but maintains distance and does not constantly interfere with them.

This was initially a visual interpretation rather than a claim about the lore.

---

# 13. Shiva — Visual Atmosphere

A later visual association emerged with the **Boreal Valley / Irithyll aesthetic from Dark Souls III**.

This introduced:

* Monumentality
* Cold architecture
* Aristocracy
* Ancient power
* Isolation
* Elegance
* Decay
* Persistence after decline

The important interpretation became:

> **A powerful kingdom in decline that still possesses its grandeur.**

This was different from simply creating a ruined kingdom.

The desired feeling was:

> **Elegant decay.**

The aesthetic could contain:

* Noble architecture
* Cold materials
* Blue and white
* Crystal
* Silver
* Subtle damage
* Cracks
* Weathering
* Declining grandeur

without becoming full horror or excessively dark fantasy.

This created a useful "soft edge" aesthetic:

> **Powerful + elegant + cold + slightly melancholic.**

---

# 14. Main-Character / Humanoid Candidates

The humanoid category eventually narrowed to:

* Y'shtola
* G'raha Tia
* Meteion

Each represented a different branding challenge.

---

# 15. Y'shtola

Y'shtola remained an important finalist because of her extremely strong recognition among FFXIV players.

The main visual identifiers discovered through the moodboard research were:

* Cat-like ears
* White hair
* Three facial markings
* Light eyes
* Long fringe
* Two defined side locks
* Medium/tan skin
* Staff
* Pendant
* Green earrings in the later black-robed appearance

Two major costume states were also relevant:

* Early white clothing
* Later black robe

The black robe created a strong visual contrast and provided additional assets:

* Green earrings
* Purple pendant
* Staff
* Black/white contrast

However, her silhouette was less distinctive when reduced to a simple shape.

A silhouette test suggested that without:

* Hair
* Ears
* Face
* Clothing

she could become relatively generic.

This made her visually weaker than Meteion in silhouette-based recognition.

Y'shtola remained important because of **cultural recognition**, but not because she offered the strongest creative challenge.

---

# 16. Meteion

Meteion emerged as the strongest alternative to Shiva.

The moodboard revealed several particularly strong visual characteristics.

### Silhouette

Meteion had a much stronger silhouette than Y'shtola.

The most important elements were:

* Wings
* Head-wing structures
* Bird-like legs
* Tail
* Compact body

The silhouette remained recognizable even without relying heavily on facial detail.

### Chibi potential

Meteion also translated extremely well into chibi form.

### Color

The blue palette was one of her strongest advantages.

Unlike Y'shtola, who relied heavily on contrast between white and black clothing, Meteion already possessed a more coherent color family.

### Assets

Research revealed:

* Pendant
* Wings
* Bird-like anatomy
* Figures
* Fanart
* Cosplay
* Chibi versions
* Toy/figure-like representations

This demonstrated that the character could survive different visual styles.

---

# 17. Meteion — Visual Interpretation

Without relying on lore, the visual interpretation initially produced:

* Innocence
* Purity
* Freedom
* Delicacy
* Fragility
* Melancholy
* Sadness
* Desire to protect
* Gender ambiguity
* Sweetness
* High fantasy
* High social status without obvious royalty

A particularly abstract impression was:

> The feeling that a race of innocent beings might have been exploited by humanity.

This was not treated as literal lore.

It was simply the visual/emotional impression produced by the design.

The emotional interpretation was later deliberately moved aside.

The concept was instead reconstructed around:

> **Freedom + Magic + Friendship**

---

# 18. Meteion — Guild Concept

The proposed direction was inspired by the general idea of a welcoming fantasy guild rather than by any specific character or power.

The guild could communicate:

* Freedom
* Friendship
* Acceptance
* Adventure
* Magic
* Harmony
* Forgiveness
* Support

Meteion would not necessarily represent the leader.

She would function as:

> **A mascot representing the guild's philosophy.**

The identity could therefore be closer to:

> A magical world where people are free to exist together, help one another and enjoy the journey.

---

# 19. G'raha Tia

G'raha became the third humanoid finalist.

The moodboard research revealed:

* Strong community affection
* Cute/funny fanart
* Strong facial expressions
* Red/gray/gold palette
* Cat ears
* Tail
* Staff
* Elaborate clothing
* Ornamental elements
* Pendant

The community frequently interprets him in:

* Cute styles
* Chibi
* Humorous situations
* More stylized anime-like art

This gave him strong adaptation potential.

However, several weaknesses appeared.

### Silhouette

His basic frontal silhouette was relatively weak.

The main identifiable elements were:

* Ears
* Tail
* Hair

Without those, the silhouette became generic.

### Facial dependence

G'raha's identity often depended heavily on expression.

The difference between:

> cute G'raha

and:

> serious G'raha

can significantly change the character's visual impression.

### Existing reinterpretation

The community already produces a large amount of stylized G'raha artwork.

This created a portfolio concern:

> **Would a new interpretation demonstrate original concept development, or primarily become another G'raha reinterpretation?**

His clothing was highly flexible, which was useful, but this flexibility could also turn into stylistic variation without a sufficiently new concept.

---

# 20. Humanoid Final Comparison

The three humanoids eventually represented three different strengths.

### Y'shtola — Recognition

Her strength was:

> **Cultural recognition.**

### G'raha Tia — Translation

His strength was:

> **Personality and community interpretation.**

### Meteion — Construction

Her strength was:

> **Building a new visual system from a relatively compact visual identity.**

The final competition therefore moved primarily toward:

> **Shiva vs. Meteion**

while G'raha and Y'shtola were removed from the final selection.

---

# 21. Portfolio-Specific Evaluation

At this stage, another metric became important:

> **How difficult is it to adapt the character into something new?**

This was not treated as a rule that:

> harder = better.

Instead, it was treated as a measure of **creative demonstration**.

If one character already has:

* A perfect mascot form
* An obvious logo
* An existing cute version
* An obvious brand identity

then producing a successful brand from it demonstrates less transformation.

Loporrit became a particularly clear example.

Its existing cute identity already provides much of the desired result.

The project therefore benefited from candidates where:

> **the designer had to make more meaningful creative decisions.**

This was especially relevant for the portfolio.

---

# 22. Final Selection Method

The final candidates were not evaluated only through abstract rankings.

They were tested through actual creative work.

The final practical exercise was deliberately small.

For each finalist, the goal was to spend approximately:

> **20 minutes**

creating a rough brand direction.

The objective was not polish.

The objective was to observe:

* Which ideas appear naturally
* Which concepts require forcing
* Which symbols emerge
* Which typography directions appear
* Which assets become available
* Which website atmosphere develops
* Which concepts remain coherent when applied

This was the final transition from:

> **analysis**

to:

> **creative validation.**

---

# 23. Meteion — Final Branding Exercise

The core words became:

> **Freedom + Magic + Friendship**

The proposed phrase was:

> **Magic in our hearts, freedom in our wings.**

## Names explored

* Free Souls
* Children of Freedom
* Free Will
* Wings of Liberty
* Flight of Freedom

## Logo exploration

Several concepts were created:

### Bird from behind

A blue bird seen from behind with emphasis on:

* Wings
* Tail
* Flight

### Side-profile bird

The bird was reduced to a flowing C/S-like shape.

This emphasized:

* Movement
* Flow
* Freedom
* Organic form

### Front-facing bird

A bird flying toward the viewer with open wings.

This provided stronger mascot potential.

### Pendant

The character's pendant was transformed into a logo.

The pendant was combined with:

* Jewel
* Wings
* Tail

This created a more abstract identity system.

The logo result was considered particularly strong.

---

# 24. Meteion — Typography and Web Test

Typography was more difficult.

A feather-like typography direction was explored, but the result became too abstract.

A lighter typographic system was considered more promising:

* Thin weight
* Open spacing
* Soft forms
* More breathing room

The website direction involved:

* Large amounts of space
* Light composition
* Magic
* Crystals
* Wind
* Organic forms
* Blue tones
* Freedom

However, the exercise revealed a major problem.

**Magic was easy to visualize.**

**Freedom was much harder.**

**Friendship was even harder.**

The danger was that the website could become:

> **Beautiful + magical + blue**

without clearly communicating:

> **Freedom + friendship + magic.**

This could result in a visually attractive brand with less conceptual depth.

---

# 25. Shiva — Final Branding Exercise

The core words became:

> **Realeza + Queda + Gelo**

The main phrase became:

> **A cold and distant reign in decline.**

This immediately produced a broader visual system.

---

# 26. Shiva — Naming

Several names were explored:

* Silent Majesty
* High Solitude
* Glacial Majesty
* Eternal Frosthold
* Royal Frostgrave
* Royal Emptiness

The two strongest directions became:

### Silent Majesty

Emphasizes:

* Distance
* Power
* Nobility
* Silence
* Refinement

### Glacial Majesty

Emphasizes:

* Ice
* Majesty
* Nobility
* Fantasy
* Power

---

# 27. Shiva — Logo

The strongest logo direction became:

> **A frozen royal crown.**

The crown was explored with:

* Defined points
* Delicate structure
* Central jewel
* Ornamental details
* Blue
* White
* Gray
* Thin lines
* Thicker structural sections

The goal was specifically to avoid a generic crown.

The internal structures could use more complex crystalline/arcane geometry.

The crown could potentially become:

* Main logo
* Simplified icon
* Jewel symbol
* Decorative pattern
* UI element

This created a stronger possibility for a complete brand system.

---

# 28. Shiva — Additional Logo Directions

Other experiments included:

### Royal pendant

A royal pendant combined with icy elements.

### Face + crown

A simplified face silhouette with:

* White hair
* Crown
* Flat facial shape

### Crown + cape

The crown was integrated into the character's cape.

This was considered less effective because the concept became more dependent on the character.

The crown alone remained the strongest direction.

---

# 29. Shiva — Typography

Typography produced more immediate possibilities than Meteion.

Directions included:

* Thin ornamental lettering
* Sharp icy terminals
* Refined forms
* Controlled serifs
* Thick icy structures
* Cracked ice
* Broken forms
* Two-typeface hierarchy

The most promising direction combined:

> **Fine ornamental detail + controlled sharpness.**

The concept of cracks could represent:

> **The fall of the kingdom**

without requiring an illustration.

This allowed the concept to enter the typography itself.

---

# 30. Shiva — Web Design

The website direction developed around:

### Structure

* More rigid
* More closed
* More geometric
* Less rounded
* Strong hierarchy
* Royal ornaments
* Solid elements

### Color

A possible progression:

> **Dark → blue → light blue**

A darker lateral region could gradually transition into the main content area.

A subtle snowfall effect could appear in the darker region.

The objective was to communicate:

> **Coldness + distance + nobility + decline**

without becoming excessively dark.

---

# 31. The Key Difference Between the Finalists

The final exercise revealed an important distinction.

## Meteion

The visual system was based primarily on:

> **Movement**

Wings → flight → wind → freedom → magic → openness.

This produced a softer, more organic brand.

However, the concepts of freedom and friendship were difficult to convert into concrete visual rules.

## Shiva

The visual system was based primarily on:

> **Structure**

Crown → sovereignty → ornament → ice → architecture → decline.

Each concept could be translated into concrete visual decisions.

This meant the identity could be built through:

* Logo
* Typography
* Assets
* Layout
* Architecture
* Color
* Materials
* Background
* Decoration

without requiring the character itself to remain visible.

---

# 32. Final Comparison

| Metric                           |     Shiva |   Meteion |
| -------------------------------- | --------: | --------: |
| Concept specificity              | Very high |      High |
| Logo potential                   |      High | Very high |
| Mascot potential                 |      High | Very high |
| Typography potential             | Very high |    Medium |
| Asset potential                  | Very high |      High |
| Web design potential             | Very high |      High |
| Visual coherence                 | Very high |      High |
| Conceptual abstraction           |    Medium | Very high |
| Risk of generic result           |       Low |    Medium |
| Adaptation opportunity           | Very high |      High |
| Portfolio demonstration          | Very high |      High |
| Ease of maintaining all concepts | Very high |    Medium |

The numbers are not intended to represent objective measurements of character quality.

They describe the result of the creative test specifically for this project.

---

# 33. Final Decision

The final choice became:

# Shiva

The decision was made primarily from the **creative process itself**.

Meteion produced a strong logo and a valid brand concept.

However, the exercise revealed that the strongest visual element of the Meteion identity was increasingly becoming:

> **Magic**

while:

> **Freedom**

and:

> **Friendship**

were more difficult to express consistently.

Shiva presented the opposite situation.

The three concepts:

> **Realeza + Queda + Gelo**

could reinforce one another throughout the identity.

For example:

> **A cracked frozen crown**

can communicate:

* Realeza
* Gelo
* Queda

simultaneously.

This gave Shiva a stronger complete visual system.

---

# 34. Why Shiva Was Chosen for the Portfolio

The final choice was not based primarily on personal preference.

In fact, personal preference was deliberately controlled throughout the final stage.

The decision was based on what the project could demonstrate.

Shiva offers opportunities to demonstrate:

* Conceptual abstraction
* Visual translation
* Symbol creation
* Logo development
* Typography
* Material language
* Color systems
* Art direction
* Web design
* Narrative through visual design
* Transformation of an existing character into an independent brand

Most importantly, the project does not need to reproduce Shiva directly.

The identity can exist independently through:

> **Crown + ornament + ice + architectural structure + decay.**

That separation between character and brand is important for the portfolio.

---

# 35. What Happened to the Other Finalists

The final selection did not mean that the other characters were poor choices.

Each finalist represented a legitimate creative opportunity.

### Y'shtola

Strongest in:

> **Recognition**

### G'raha Tia

Strongest in:

> **Community attachment and personality**

### Meteion

Strongest in:

> **Mascot construction and visual world-building**

### Shiva

Strongest in:

> **Transformation into a complete visual identity**

The final choice therefore reflected the project's specific objective rather than a universal ranking of the characters.

---

# 36. Final Lesson From the Selection Process

The selection process changed the project's central question several times.

### Initial question

> **Which character has the best visual potential?**

### After Calcabrina

> **Which character can actually work for the intended audience?**

### After the candidate groups

> **Which characters are strong enough visually and culturally to support a guild?**

### After the moodboards

> **What makes each character recognizable and adaptable?**

### At the portfolio stage

> **Which character allows the designer to demonstrate the most meaningful design decisions?**

The current finalists therefore were not simply the most popular or visually attractive characters.

They represented different forms of creative opportunity:

> **Shiva — transform.**
> Transform an existing character language into an original visual system.

> **Meteion — build.**
> Build a new magical and community-oriented visual world from a compact mascot identity.

> **G'raha — translate.**
> Translate an established personality and strong community interpretation into a coherent brand without relying on existing fan interpretations.

The final decision should therefore consider not only which character would make the best guild, but:

> **Which resulting project best demonstrates the designer's ability to research, think, conceptualize, adapt, build and execute a complete visual identity from beginning to end?**

For this specific objective, **Shiva was selected**.

The project now moves from **character selection** into **brand development**.

The character is no longer the final product.

It is the starting point for creating an original guild identity.
